FIX #1: RE-INTEGRATE CONVERTED SQL STATEMENTS WITH PROPER SCHEMA NAMES
================================================================================

Issue Description:
- ProductRepository.cs contained unqualified table names (Products, ProductHistory, ProductStats)
- DMS tool converted these to schema-qualified names (productmanagement_dbo.products, etc.)
- All column names were converted to lowercase by DMS
- Original SQL not properly replaced with converted SQL from converted_statements.sql

Methods Fixed:
1. GetAllProductsAsync
2. GetProductByIdAsync
3. InsertProductAsync
4. UpdateProductAsync
5. DeleteProductAsync
6. GetProductsByPriceRangeAsync
7. GetLowStockProductsAsync

Changes Applied:

Method: GetAllProductsAsync
- Changed: "FROM Products" → "FROM productmanagement_dbo.products"
- Changed: All column references to lowercase (productid, name, price, etc.)
- Changed: CTE name "ProductStats" → "productstats"
- Added: "NULLS FIRST" to ORDER BY clauses for PostgreSQL consistency

Method: GetProductByIdAsync
- Changed: "FROM Products" → "FROM productmanagement_dbo.products"
- Changed: All column references to lowercase
- Changed: CTE name "ProductHistory" → "producthistory"
- Changed: "LEFT JOIN" → "LEFT OUTER JOIN" (as per DMS conversion)

Method: GetProductsByPriceRangeAsync
- Changed: "FROM Products" → "FROM productmanagement_dbo.products"
- Changed: All column references to lowercase
- Changed: CTE name "RankedProducts" → "rankedproducts"
- Added: "NULLS FIRST" to ORDER BY

Method: GetLowStockProductsAsync
- Changed: "FROM Products" → "FROM productmanagement_dbo.products"
- Changed: All column references to lowercase
- Changed: CTE name "StockAnalysis" → "stockanalysis"
- Added: "NULLS FIRST" to ORDER BY

================================================================================
FIX #2: IMPLEMENT PROPER TRANSACTION MANAGEMENT AT ADO.NET LEVEL
================================================================================

Issue Description:
- Statements 3, 4, 5 (InsertProductAsync, UpdateProductAsync, DeleteProductAsync) 
  contained embedded SQL transaction blocks (BEGIN TRANSACTION...COMMIT)
- PostgreSQL does not support these embedded transaction commands in functions
- DMS tool issued warning [7807 - Severity CRITICAL] about this issue
- Transaction management must be handled at ADO.NET level using NpgsqlTransaction

Changes Applied:

Method: InsertProductAsync
BEFORE:
- Single SQL string with BEGIN TRANSACTION...COMMIT embedded
- Used DECLARE @NewProductId variable (SQL Server syntax)
- TODO comment indicating RETURNING clause needs implementation

AFTER:
- Split into 3 separate SQL commands
- Added: using var transaction = await connection.BeginTransactionAsync()
- Command 1: INSERT with RETURNING productid to get new ID
- Command 2: INSERT into producthistory using returned ID
- Command 3: UPDATE productstats
- All commands use the same transaction object
- try-catch block with CommitAsync() on success, RollbackAsync() on error
- Proper schema names: productmanagement_dbo.products, productmanagement_dbo.producthistory, productmanagement_dbo.productstats
- All column names in lowercase

Method: UpdateProductAsync
BEFORE:
- Single SQL string with BEGIN TRANSACTION...COMMIT embedded
- DECLARE variables for @OldPrice and @OldStock
- All operations in one SQL batch

AFTER:
- Split into 4 separate commands within ADO.NET transaction
- Added: using var transaction = await connection.BeginTransactionAsync()
- Command 1: SELECT to get old values (oldPrice, oldStock)
- Command 2: UPDATE product record
- Command 3: INSERT history record
- Command 4: UPDATE statistics
- All commands use the same transaction object
- try-catch block with CommitAsync() on success, RollbackAsync() on error
- Added error handling: throws InvalidOperationException if product not found
- Changed: GETDATE() → clock_timestamp() (as per DMS conversion)
- Proper schema names and lowercase columns

Method: DeleteProductAsync
BEFORE:
- Single SQL string with BEGIN TRANSACTION...COMMIT embedded
- DECLARE variables for @OldPrice and @OldStock
- All operations in one SQL batch

AFTER:
- Split into 4 separate commands within ADO.NET transaction
- Added: using var transaction = await connection.BeginTransactionAsync()
- Command 1: SELECT to get old values for history
- Command 2: INSERT history record (before deletion)
- Command 3: DELETE product record
- Command 4: UPDATE statistics
- All commands use the same transaction object
- try-catch block with CommitAsync() on success, RollbackAsync() on error
- Added error handling: throws InvalidOperationException if product not found
- Changed: GETDATE() → clock_timestamp()
- Changed: NOW() → clock_timestamp() for consistency with DMS conversion
- Proper schema names and lowercase columns

